{
  "_comment": [
    "Extensions loaded by the broker at startup, listed here rather than discovered by scanning a",
    "directory: a broker that loads whatever is in a folder behaves according to what someone left",
    "there.",
    "",
    "This is a template. Copy it to xmq_extensions.conf beside xmq_server.conf and edit it. Nothing",
    "installs the live file, and a broker without one simply has no extensions - which is not an",
    "error and is the normal case.",
    "",
    "It is a separate file rather than a section of xmq_server.conf because that file is generated",
    "from xmq.wsdl and rewritten from that model, so a section the model does not know about would",
    "be dropped the first time anything was saved. Accounts live in their own file for the same",
    "reason.",
    "",
    "'name' must match the name the library declares, or the broker refuses to load it - otherwise",
    "this file names one extension and the broker runs another. Everything under 'settings' belongs",
    "to the extension and means whatever its documentation says it means.",
    "",
    "There are two places the broker reads: this file, and xmq_extensions.d beside it. Every",
    "*.conf in that directory is read after this file, in name order - hence the convention of a",
    "numeric prefix, 10-, 50-, since the order extensions are asked in is part of what they mean.",
    "It exists so that an extension can install its own entry instead of asking somebody to merge",
    "one into this file by hand, and so that installing a second extension does not touch the",
    "first one's. A fragment holds the same {\"extensions\": [ ... ]} shape as this file.",
    "",
    "'required' says whether the broker may serve MQTT without this extension. It defaults to",
    "false, which is the rule the broker has always had: a broken extension does not stop it. Set",
    "it true for an extension that is the only way clients are authenticated - otherwise a library",
    "that fails to load leaves the broker with no authentication at all and nothing that says so.",
    "A required extension that does not start stops the MQTT listeners, and only those: the",
    "configuration interface still comes up, which is where the fault is repaired from.",
    "",
    "'library' is a full path and the broker loads exactly it. Keep extensions out of lib/xmq: that",
    "is the directory the web interface is served from, so anything left there can be fetched over",
    "HTTP by anyone who can reach the control service. lib/xmq-extensions is not served.",
    "",
    "All three entries below are the shipped samples, and all are switched off. Their sources are",
    "in share/xmq/examples/extensions and are public domain: copy one to start your own."
  ],
  "extensions": [
    {
      "_comment": "Writes every broker event to a file. Declares XMQ_CAP_OBSERVER, so it is never asked about a connection.",
      "name": "event-log",
      "library": "/usr/local/lib/xmq-extensions/libxmq_event_log.so",
      "enabled": false,
      "settings": {
        "file": "/var/log/xmq/events.log"
      }
    },
    {
      "_comment": "Authenticates against the broker's own SQL user database - the tables the Users screen edits - so an account added there can connect a second later, on any node sharing the database. Declares XMQ_CAP_AUTHENTICATOR. Set 'required' to true where this is the only way clients are authenticated: without it a library that fails to load leaves the broker admitting nobody and saying nothing about why. Where that database is comes from the broker, not from here: the interface writes to the broker's address, and a second copy of it in this block could be pointed elsewhere - every account added in the interface would then be saved, listed, and admit nobody. Set it with 'database_uri' in the broker's own authentication section. 'unknown_user' is 'deny' by default, because this extension is the account database and a name it does not have is a name that does not exist; set it to 'abstain' to stack this with a directory that may know the client instead.",
      "name": "user-database",
      "library": "/usr/local/lib/xmq-extensions/libxmq_user_database.so",
      "enabled": false,
      "required": false,
      "settings": {
        "unknown_user": "deny"
      }
    },
    {
      "_comment": "Admits the listed client ids and abstains on everyone else, leaving them to the broker's own accounts. Declares XMQ_CAP_AUTHENTICATOR, so it is never handed an event. 'lookup_ms' stands in for the directory round trip a real one makes.",
      "name": "allow-list",
      "library": "/usr/local/lib/xmq-extensions/libxmq_allow_list.so",
      "enabled": false,
      "settings": {
        "allow": "sensor-1, sensor-2, gateway",
        "lookup_ms": "0"
      }
    },
    {
      "_comment": "Lets a group use the topics under its own name: 'ops-alice' and 'ops-bob' are both group 'ops', and both may use site/ops/... and nothing else. Declares XMQ_CAP_AUTHORIZER. Permissions belong to the group, never to the client - that is what lets the broker cache each answer per group and topic and keep the extension off the message path.",
      "name": "topic-guard",
      "library": "/usr/local/lib/xmq-extensions/libxmq_topic_guard.so",
      "enabled": false,
      "settings": {
        "separator": "-",
        "root": "site"
      }
    }
  ]
}
